Ray Dillinger wrote:
> 
> Early versions of Nethack had "multiple keys" or "random keys"
> on the theory that "different keys open different doors" and
> in order to open a door with a key, you had to go through your
> collection of keys trying each in turn until you found the "right"
> one.  I think there were a total of 8 different keys.
> 
> This sucked, and players everywhere cried out for it to be removed,
> and lo, it was removed.  Currently Nethack has exactly one type of
> key - the skeleton key - and it opens every locked door in the
> dungeon.
> 
> If you want to introduce a "multiple-keys" feature that doesn't
> suck, you have to address the problems that caused this feature
> to suck from a gameplay perspective.
> 
> First, the player (if not the character) must be spared the tedium
> of trying each and every damned key. There has to be a keyboard
> command that means "try my keys to open this door."  The player,
> encountering a locked door, types this single keystroke command.
> The result is:
> 
> 
> "The door opens with the Xth key" where X is some number from 1
> to the number of keys currently in the player's posession and the
> time taken is (3+x)/4 rounds or some similar expression,  OR
> 
> "none of your keys open this door"  OR
> 
> "You remember this door.  It opens with the Xth key." where X is
> the same number as the key it opened with last time, and the time
> taken is as for trying exactly one key.
> 
> 
> 
> Second, in a game with limited inventory slots, the character
> should not be spending an inventory slot on each key.  Different
> keys must "stack" up to and including the total number of different
> keys in the system.  One of your inventory slots is "a keyring with
> N different keys on it" that weighs ((6 + X)/6)s where X is the
> number of keys. If your game has a bulk/weight inventory system
> instead, this is a non-problem in the first place since keys are
> so small you can carry thousands of them in a backpack.
> 
> I'm not going to go into details and decisions like whether locking
> a door requires a key, whether it can be done with a skeleton key,
> how and whether to implement "master keys" that can be used instead
> of some subset of random keys, how broadly applicable are lockpicks,
> etc.  These are all subgames that can add interest and
> microoptimization opportunities, but none of them is really a
> make-or-break for the system and whether random keys are good to
> have in the first place.
> 
>                     Bear
