A single key may be OK for the Nethack dungeons but would it be feasible 
for roguelikes with different kinds of maps? For example, in a game with 
an overworld, 10 different cities populated by 5 different races, some 
villages, dungeons, mines and a castle it sounds a bit simplistic for 
just one key to open all doors.

The best way is to have multiple keys organized in the inventory as 
described above. The 'try all keys' sounds like Rogue's "'F'ight to 
death" and is very reasonable.

Of course there shouldn't exist many keys, 3-6 is enough. Also, they 
shouldn't be just random, a player should expect a theme - doors could 
have the same locks (or a variation of 2 locks) in the same dungeon 
level or the same gnome village.

And there should exist (rare) locks that can't open with keys but in 
other ways (lockpicking, kicking the door down, saying a magic word, 
etc.), so that item-harvesting dosn't make it trivial.

